rules_version = '2';

// HazeHub Firestore security rules.
//
// Every read and write is DENIED. That is deliberate and it is the whole
// security model:
//
//   * The only thing that touches Firestore is the HazeHub API (Cloud Run),
//     which uses the Firebase Admin SDK. The Admin SDK authenticates as a
//     service account and bypasses security rules entirely, so denying
//     everything here costs the app nothing.
//   * No browser, phone or third-party client ever holds a Firestore
//     credential, so there is no client traffic to allow. Scouters
//     authenticate to the API with a scouter ID + the shared team password
//     and get an HMAC token; the API enforces admin-only routes.
//   * It is also the quota control. Sixty devices reading Firestore directly
//     would cost millions of document reads a day; routing everything
//     through the API's in-memory cache is what keeps the app inside the
//     free tier. These rules make the expensive path impossible by
//     construction, not merely discouraged.
//
// If you ever add a client that talks to Firestore directly, add a narrow
// rule for exactly those paths — and re-do the quota math first.

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
